# 2. Translation Proof

This should probably be one of the flagship products.

Not:

> “AI says this translation is good.”

But:

```text
TRANSLATION
    ↓
structured audit dimensions
    ↓
TRANSLATION PROOF
```

Your current dimensions are already close:

```text
SOURCE_COVERAGE
TARGET_GROUNDING
MORPHOLOGY
SYNTAX
NEGATION
MODALITY
TERM_CONSISTENCY
SEMANTIC_ENTAILMENT
PARALLEL_WITNESS
HUMAN_REVIEW
```

Expose the proof as a **non-aggregate vector**.

Example:

```text
Translation Proof TP-0093

Coverage             PASS
Morphology           PASS
Syntax               PASS
Negation             PASS
Modality             WARN
Terminology          PASS
Entailment            PASS
Parallel witness     2 found
Independent review   PASS
Scholar review       pending
```

This is much stronger than a “94% translation quality score.”

Checkpoint:

```text
TP0 generated
TP1 deterministic checks
TP2 adversarial audit
TP3 independent review
TP4 scholar adjudicated
```

---

# 3. Passage / Reading

This is the philology-facing primitive.

For every canonical passage:

```text
SOURCE
├── witnesses
├── editions
├── variants
├── segmentation
├── morphology
├── readings
└── translation revisions
```

Product:

> **Passage Workbench**

This is where a Sanskritist can say:

```text
I disagree with this sandhi resolution.
I prefer reading X.
This edition has Y.
```

Checkpoint:

```text
P0 ingested
P1 normalized
P2 witness-aligned
P3 readings encoded
P4 reviewed
```

This becomes the foundation underneath Translation Proof.

